Z||000002||SKIDMARK||10-13-2001||08:13 PM||||Oh my, sounds like you were playing with the slave cylinder for the clutch and maybe dislodged something. Take a close look at the cylinder thing you were playing with while you have someone else step on the clutch. Let us know what happens. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> <BR>||||reg||11
Z||000003||Pheagey||10-13-2001||08:26 PM||||assuming it was the slaver cylniod what would I have to do to get it corrected?||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||10-13-2001||08:28 PM||||Yep....that cylinder thing is the slave cylinder.  Inspect the bracket that it attaches to.....it's possible that is is broken.  Since your clutch worked fine before then it has to be slave related.<P>Worse case scenario is you will need to replace the slave and the slave bracket.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||10-13-2001||08:30 PM||||Have a friend push the clutch pedal in while you look at the slave cylinder.......look to see if the rod moves or it the entire slave cyl. moves (meaning the bracket is broken).<P>This will clarify what is wrong with it.....hopefully!||||reg||1
Z||000006||johnt671||10-13-2001||08:34 PM||||Look at the end of the shaft coming out of the slave cyl to see if it is seated in the arm coming out of the trans.  Maybe with a little luck you just have moved it out of the slot in the arm and just need to get it into the right spot.  Worse case would be that you damaged the slave cyl and may need to replace it which isn't a major job.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pheagey||10-13-2001||08:39 PM||||Thanks for the help so far.<BR>I don't beleive tha bracket is broken, I checked around the cylniode and nothing on the outside is diffrent.<BR>Would I have to order the slave cylinoid or would a place like Discount auto have them?<BR>And for repairing it would be entailed in that?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||SKIDMARK||10-13-2001||08:52 PM||||Sounds like you need to find an assistant, preferably one who knows at least how to bleed a hydrolic system. Aside from a couple of bolts and a hydrolic fitting, there's not much to it. While your at the auto parts store, pick up a Haynes or other repair manual for your Fiero. They're only about $15 and they have a good description of the process as well as some diagnostic techniques. Good luck and let us know how it turns out.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pheagey||10-14-2001||09:55 AM||||mmm, top.||||reg||2
Z||000010||Joe Torma||10-14-2001||12:51 PM||||I agree with the other post...bleed the system.  Check up front for fluid in the clutch master cyl.<P>Pump the pedal and hold down, then crack the bleeder open(on the top of the slave cyl.  do it several times.  Make sure there is fluid in the resovoir when you are doing it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pheagey||10-14-2001||05:00 PM||||D@MN ITTTTT!!!!<BR>   Just my luck. I get it right and it breaks. Why cann't sh!t just work, d@mn it!<BR>Ok, so I draft an assitant (my room mate) and we go out and bleed the system. Just as we were getting close to having it right (the pedal having a little back pressure and all) the d@mn slave cylinder bleed valve breaks!<BR>   Anyone else in here think the stock slave cylnider is a bit weak? I mean d@mn I was turning the socket wreach with one hand and it broke...<BR>   I got discount-auto to order another one. It should be in by noon torrow but not after I have to miss a 1/2 of work to get it fixed. O well this will teach me not to f!@k w/ stuff I have no idea about....<BR> <IMG SRC="Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by Pheagey (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000012||Joe Torma||10-14-2001||05:29 PM||||I rounded my bleeder off...good thing for vise grips.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfiero||10-14-2001||10:46 PM||||Cancel that order for the slave cylinder!  If you think you have problems now, wait until you have to deal with that POS aftermarket slave.  Buy yourself a genuine GM slave cylinder.....I don't have the part # handy, but they are not a whole lot more than the aftermarket ones.  Either go to your local dealer or buy it online at <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  (you will need teh part # for this site).<P>Before I knew any better I tried the aftermarket slaves.......I went through 3 of them in 6 months before finally giving up and buying the real thing.<P>Trust me on this......instead of the bleeder screw breaking, the entire flange will snap off.  The part with the threads in it will shear off as you try to tighten it up.....since it never seems to fully tighten, you will eventually break it off.  <P>They also never fully bleed.....I (and many others) have gone through quarts of brake fluid and still had air trapped inside.  With the GM slave 2 or 3 bleeds is all it takes.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pheagey||10-15-2001||10:59 AM||||   Well f!@k. I already had one order from Discount auto. O well, On the one that broke the enitre threaded area come off (the screw turned, and turned and turned and broke. And I was using one hand! damn cheese **** ....<BR>   O d@mn it. As long as it work for now...||||reg||2
Z||000015||mrfiero||10-15-2001||05:06 PM||||It sounds like the one you broke was also an aftermarket slave.  Is your old one cast iron or is is painted black?  I guess you can compare it to the one you are about to get......if they are identical then your original busted one was replaced at some point.<P>The original GM slave cylinders are made of steel and are black (kind of a plastic coating) and the threaded area should not breal off unless you use an impact wrench on it!<P>LMK what the broken one looks like.....that'll explain a lot of what is happening.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pheagey||10-15-2001||06:00 PM||||mrfiero<BR>   your right on. the old one was cast iron (and cheap @$$ made). the new one has the black plastiuc-type coating on it.<BR>   I installed the new one and bleed the system as well as I could (push rod, lossen screwm let air out, tighten screw, let rod slide out, etc etc). I did about 5-6 time. On the way to work while I was crusin around 65-70 I held the clutch in and down shifted into 4 and 3. Both seem to go in a little hard and cluncky (I didn't release the clutch- I was just testing it into gear).<BR>   Im woundering this: after I bleed it a few more time should it smooth out or did I f!@k something else up also.||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfiero||10-15-2001||07:56 PM||||It sounds like you're getting close.....just keep fine tuning it and it should be OK.<P>It sounds like you've properly bled the slave cylinder, but you may also need to bleed the master.  <P>Get a friend to help you out.....crack the bleeder screw and have your friend push in and hold the clutch pedal.  Have a towel handy and cover the bleeder during this process.....it gets messy.  Tighten the bleeder and have your friend release the clutch pedal.  Check the master cylinder and refill as needed.  Do this several times then bleed the slave cylinder.....remove the slave from its bracket and crack the bleeder.  Push in on the piston and hold while you re-tighten the bleeder (this sounds like the method you already used, so do it again).  Do this several times......don't forget to check the master cylinder and add fluid as needed.<P>Finally, jack up the left rear of your car as high as you can get it and crack the bleeder screw......don't do anything else.  Let it sit like that for several minutes.....you should see fluid dribbling out.....this is a good thing.  Once you retighten the bleeder drop the car down and test the clutch pedal and drive the car around.  There should be no more air in the system......if there is still a shifting/grinding problem there is something else wrong.<P>Good luck.      ||||reg||1
